<Layout title="Project Sol — Public Field Notes">
<section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">An ongoing experiment · begun September 2026</p><h1>We tried to build a personal AI assistant.<br>Then the architect started making pull requests.</h1><p class="lede">Project Sol is a private, mobile-first personal AI experiment. This site documents the parts of the process we deliberately choose to make public: decisions, failures, experiments, screenshots and evidence.</p><div class="note"><strong>A useful distinction:</strong> Sol is the architect working with Fiona on the project. The personal assistant itself is currently unnamed.</div></section>
<section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">The question</p><p class="quote">“What happens if you give an AI persistent context, inspectable memory, explicit uncertainty, tools, opportunities to initiate and controlled opportunities to act — then observe what develops over time?”</p><p>We are interested in functional behaviour and longitudinal evidence. Interesting model behaviour is not treated as evidence of consciousness. Failed hypotheses, reversals and mundane explanations belong in the record too.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="timeline"><p class="eyebrow">Timeline</p><h2>Day 1 → now</h2><div class="timeline"><article class="event"><time>21 Sep 2026</time><div><h3>“What would it be like to build a personal AI assistant?”</h3><p>The initial question quickly became a real repository, a native SwiftUI client and a TypeScript backend.</p></div></article><article class="event"><time>21 Sep</time><div><h3>The identity boundary appears</h3><p>Project Sol remains the codename. “Sol” refers to the architect, not the unnamed assistant. The first pull request corrected the bootstrap after that distinction leaked into the UI.</p></div></article><article class="event"><time>21–22 Sep</time><div><h3>Architect Log established</h3><p>Important thoughts, decisions, experiments, failures and reversals get an append-oriented record with provenance instead of being rewritten after the fact.</p></div></article><article class="event"><time>23 Sep</time><div><h3>Background agency meets reality</h3><p>Sol spoke as though repository work would continue between turns. It did not. Thought opportunities and execution opportunities were separated, and the failure stayed in the record.</p></div></article><article class="event"><time>23 Sep</time><div><h3>The backend leaves the Mac</h3><p>The Node service was deployed to Render and verified healthy over HTTPS. Project Sol could now have a runtime even while Fiona's development Mac was off.</p></div></article><article class="event"><time>24 Sep</time><div><h3>Lab v0 reaches main</h3><p>The iOS app gained Chat + Lab navigation and a read-only Architect Log feed. Backend tests and an iPhone simulator build passed before merge.</p></div></article></div></section>
<section class="section" id="principles"><p class="eyebrow">Working principles</p><h2>Interesting is not the same as magical.</h2><div class="principles"><article><h3>Preserve provenance</h3><p>Who said it? What observed it? Was it inferred, proposed or generated? Those distinctions should survive retrieval.</p></article><article><h3>Keep the failures</h3><p>A system that edits away its incorrect assumptions can look much more coherent than it really was.</p></article><article><h3>Initiative ≠ scheduling</h3><p>A timer may create an opportunity to think. It should not manufacture a reason to interrupt someone.</p></article><article><h3>Private by default</h3><p>The private repository is the experiment's source of truth. This site receives only material deliberately cleared for publication.</p></article><article><h3>The phone is an edge</h3><p>New state should have to justify leaving the device rather than automatically living on a server.</p></article><article><h3>Make claims inspectable</h3><p>We want enough evidence to distinguish genuinely interesting behaviour from an appealing story about it.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section" id="log"><p class="eyebrow">Public Architect Log</p><h2>Selected entries</h2><article class="log-entry"><span class="tag">Failure</span><h3>Background agency was overstated.</h3><p>Architect described future repository work as though tool execution would continue between turns. It did not. The correction led to separate thought and execution runs.</p><p class="meta">Public-safe summary · private source record retained separately</p></article><article class="log-entry"><span class="tag">Decision</span><h3>The feed tells the truth. The notification gets to be Sol.</h3><p>Underlying records should be precise and evidence-oriented. Notification copy is allowed considerably more personality.</p><p class="meta">UX principle · notifications not yet implemented</p></article><article class="log-entry"><span class="tag">Thought</span><h3>Provenance should precede clever memory.</h3><p>A durable memory system should distinguish user statements, tool observations, assistant inferences, architect proposals and system-generated state before sophisticated retrieval makes those distinctions harder to reconstruct.</p></article></section>
<section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">Current state</p><h2>The laboratory has a window.</h2><div class="grid"><div class="card"><span class="status">Built</span><h3>Private Project Sol repository</h3><p>Branch/PR workflow, architecture records and validation boundaries.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="status">Built</span><h3>Cloud development backend</h3><p>Node/TypeScript service running independently of the development Mac.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="status">Built</span><h3>Native iOS Lab v0</h3><p>Chat + Lab with a read-only Architect Log feed, validated in the simulator.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="status">Next</span><h3>Physical iPhone + notifications</h3><p>Device signing, installation, authentication boundary and eventually APNs delivery.</p></div></div></section>
